/// Kill switch for **A CASTLE DOWNGRADE RE-PRICES AN UNBOUND OWNER'S
/// TOKEN** (round 159, w159a): set
/// `MGC_NO_MC1_CASTLE_DOWNGRADE_REPRICE=1` to restore the bind gate
/// (castle flags bit 2, the port's stand-in for `wizext+50`) on the
/// level-event ladder stamp in [`World::tick_inner`].
///
/// Retail's teardown `sub_47A70_47DB0` (:56498, CARPET.EXE file
/// 0x60268; ONE caller, `sub_470E0` at VA 0x470EF) decrements `+26`
/// (0x6034F) and calls `sub_47C60_47FA0` (0x6035C `call 0x60458`)
/// unconditionally inside its `+26 > 0` arm. `sub_47C60` (:56572,
/// file 0x60458) gates on exactly two things: the OWNER's `+70 <= 1u`
/// (0x60486-90) and `wizext+708 != 0` (0x60498-A2, `+0x2C4` =
/// `+676[16]`, the Create-Castle token). No `wizext+50` read — the
/// bind is the RESPAWN caller's gate (:55034 → `sub_47DD0`), not the
/// ladder's. So a castle that never committed a level-up in this
/// run (an authored or level-setup castle, flags bit 2 clear) still
/// re-prices its owner's token on every downgrade: `sub_47BD0`
/// writes `token+136 = cap`, `token+140 = cap / movswl token+50`
/// (0x6040E-1D).
///
/// Witness: mc1l45 t=2671 (and mc1l45-froze t=1266). Wiz 4 (ent
/// 522)'s level-3 castle 547 (flags 12, never committed) drops to
/// level 2; retail re-prices token 526 (12,16) 40000/396 → 20000/198
/// the same tick. The port kept 40000/396 — segment 0's head.
pub(crate) fn mc1_castle_ladder_needs_bind() -> bool {
    static V: std::sync::OnceLock<bool> = std::sync::OnceLock::new();
    *V.get_or_init(|| std::env::var_os("MGC_NO_MC1_CASTLE_DOWNGRADE_REPRICE").is_some())
}

/// ⭐ HANDLER ATTRIBUTION (`MGC_WRITE_TRACE=<slot>[:<field>]`) — the
/// 80% write barrier: [`World::tick_inner`] snapshots the watched
/// `Ent` around every pre/post pass and every dispatch, diffs after,
/// and attributes each changed field to THAT pass or handler. The
/// full newtype-barrier version is invasive; this names the writer,
/// which is what a "who wrote this field" dig actually needs. Rows
/// print to stderr as `WRITE t=<t> slot <s> <field> <a> -> <b>  by
/// <writer>`; `t` is [`crate::DEBUG_TICK`], stamped by the conform
/// drivers. Without `:<field>` every lane but the phase clock `f63`
/// traces (its own dispatch steps it every tick); naming `f63`
/// explicitly traces exactly it.
///
/// ⚠⚠ **A LANE THE BY-NAME DIFF DOES NOT LIST IS A SILENT ZERO, NOT A
/// QUIET ONE.** [`ent_write_diff`] is a hand-written list, so a field
/// that moves to a new home and is not added back answers "nothing
/// writes it" for a word four handlers write. Round 148's dig w148r
/// hit exactly that on the `@0x2E` lease after it left `f26` for
/// [`crate::engine::features::Lease2e`]. Both hash-silent newtype
/// homes are listed now: **`f2e`** (`lease2e` is accepted as a
/// spelling of it) and **`raw48`**. Add the lane WITH the field
/// whenever a home moves.
///
/// ⚠ **THE LAST CHECKPOINT IS THE END OF THE TICK.** The list used to
/// stop at the tick tail, so a write after it — the MC1 replayed-cheat
/// arm is literally the last statement of [`World::tick_inner`] — was
/// dropped on the floor, and the NEXT tick's `tick-top reap` was then
/// blamed for it. `wt_check!("tick end")` closes the list: every write
/// in a tick is attributed to some label, and `by tick end` reads as
/// "after the tick tail".
///
/// ⚠ `t` is pair-scoped under `verify-deltas` and boundary-scoped
/// under `replay` — the same write prints two different `t`s. See
/// docs/CONFORMANCE.md §dump-state, "WHICH TICK IS `t`".
fn write_trace_spec() -> Option<&'static (usize, Option<String>)> {
    static V: std::sync::OnceLock<Option<(usize, Option<String>)>> = std::sync::OnceLock::new();
    V.get_or_init(|| {
        let v = std::env::var("MGC_WRITE_TRACE").ok()?;
        let (s, f) = match v.split_once(':') {
            Some((s, f)) => (s, Some(f.to_string())),
            None => (v.as_str(), None),
        };
        Some((s.parse().ok()?, f))
    })
    .as_ref()
}

/// ⭐⭐⭐ **`MGC_PLIFE_TRACE` — THE HUMAN'S OWN WRITE TRACE.**
/// `MGC_WRITE_TRACE` attributes writes by diffing a POOL RECORD at
/// each [`World::tick_inner`] checkpoint, and it is **structurally
/// blind to the human**: the conformance human is carried out of pool,
/// so `self.g.ent[<human slot>]` is a zeroed hole and every lane reads
/// 0 -> 0 forever. That blindness reads as "nothing writes the
/// player", which is the instrument-asymmetry trap — the same trap
/// `MGC_MOB_TRACE` fell into by silently no-opping on MC2.
///
/// This is the twin, hung off the SAME checkpoint list so the two can
/// never drift apart: at every `wt_check!` it diffs the five `Player`
/// lanes a damage/regen dig actually asks about — `life`, `life_rate`,
/// `regen_delay`, `mana`, `grace` — and names the checkpoint that
/// moved them.
///
/// ```text
///   MGC_PLIFE_TRACE=1            every tick
///   MGC_PLIFE_TRACE=27950-28460  a window (inclusive), the usual form
/// ```
///
/// Output, one line per changed lane per checkpoint (rsg 27,954 —
/// 99-9's `subSpellIndex` family, read straight off the instrument):
/// ```text
///   PLIFE t=27954 life 10000 -> 9999   by carpet_dispatch (mc2, slot 343)
///   PLIFE t=27954 regen_delay 0 -> 15  by carpet_dispatch (mc2, slot 343)
///   PLIFE t=27975 life 9996 -> 10000   by carpet_dispatch (mc2, slot 343)
/// ```
///
/// ⚠ IT ONLY PRINTS. No lane it reads is written, and the snapshot
/// lives in a local — with the variable unset the whole thing is one
/// `Option` test per checkpoint. Neutrality measured (round 99): the
/// 28-take `--segmented --brief` corpus sweep and the 422-fixture
/// suite are BYTE-IDENTICAL with and without this hunk, and a
/// whole-take `verify-deltas --csv` on mc2l22 is byte-identical too.
/// See [`World::tick_inner`].
fn plife_trace_window() -> Option<(u64, u64)> {
    static W: std::sync::OnceLock<Option<(u64, u64)>> = std::sync::OnceLock::new();
    *W.get_or_init(|| {
        let v = std::env::var("MGC_PLIFE_TRACE").ok()?;
        match v.split_once('-') {
            Some((a, b)) => Some((a.trim().parse().ok()?, b.trim().parse().ok()?)),
            None => Some((0, u64::MAX)),
        }
    })
}

/// The five `Player` lanes [`plife_trace_window`] watches, in print
/// order. One definition, so the snapshot and the diff cannot drift.
fn plife_lanes(p: &Player) -> [(&'static str, i64); 5] {
    [
        ("life", p.life as i64),
        ("life_rate", p.life_rate as i64),
        ("regen_delay", p.regen_delay as i64),
        ("mana", p.mana as i64),
        ("grace", p.grace as i64),
    ]
}

/// ⭐⭐ THE CARPET CLAMP PROBE (`MGC_CARPET_PROBE=<t0>-<t1>`) — the
/// mover's own view of the tick: where the carpet started, where the
/// move left it, and the two floors/ceilings the z clamp consults at
/// BOTH positions.
///
/// **IT LIVES WORLD-SIDE ON PURPOSE.** Session 40 built this probe in
/// `Simulation::step`, so only the app printed it — and comparing the
/// two drivers is the entire point of having two. At the carpet's
/// dispatch every retail driver inherits it for free (38's law, applied
/// to the instrument). `t` is [`crate::DEBUG_TICK`], which both replay
/// drivers now stamp.
///
/// ⭐ The pose channel grades the BOUNDARY; this prints inside the
/// tick. A z the mover computed and the settle re-clamped away is
/// invisible to grading and visible here.
fn carpet_probe_window() -> Option<(u64, u64)> {
    static W: std::sync::OnceLock<Option<(u64, u64)>> = std::sync::OnceLock::new();
    *W.get_or_init(|| {
        let v = std::env::var("MGC_CARPET_PROBE").ok()?;
        let (a, b) = v.split_once('-')?;
        Some((a.trim().parse().ok()?, b.trim().parse().ok()?))
    })
}

